## The Single vs Double Decision

This is the biggest decision after the stone. It changes the whole character of the ring.

A single halo puts one row of diamonds around the center stone. The radiant cut is clearly the hero. The halo adds about 30–40% apparent size and a ring of brilliance that frames the stone without overshadowing it.

A double halo adds a second outer ring of diamonds. The center stone is still visible, but it's now nested inside two glittering frames. The ring as a whole becomes the statement — not just the stone.

Most buyers choose single halo. It's cleaner, more timeless, and the center stone stays prominent. Double halo is the right choice if you want maximum drama and you're comfortable with a bolder, more fashion-forward look.

One more thing to know: double halos add diamond weight. The setting price goes up by $500–$1,500 over a single halo. That same money could go toward a bigger or better center stone instead.

